#Order Processing Microservices

An event-driven order processing backend built with Java 21, Spring Boot, Spring Cloud, RabbitMQ, and MySQL, deployed to AWS ECS Fargate with automated GitHub Actions CI/CD.
The application supports customer registration and JWT authentication, order creation, simulated payment processing, automatic order status updates, persistent notifications, and transactional emails delivered through Amazon SES.
Project status: Complete for the current portfolio scope. Deployed to AWS ECS Fargate, with a successful GitHub Actions CI/CD deployment, 29 passing Order Service automated tests, and a verified Postman end-to-end order workflow. Payments are simulated; no real money is charged.
Tech Stack
Area	Technologies
Language & framework	Java 21, Spring Boot, Maven
Security	Spring Security, JWT
Microservices	Spring Cloud Gateway, Netflix Eureka, Spring Cloud Config
Messaging	RabbitMQ
Persistence	Spring Data JPA, MySQL
Email	Spring Mail, Amazon Simple Email Service (SES) via SMTP
Containers	Docker, Docker Compose
AWS infrastructure	ECS Fargate, ECR, RDS MySQL, CloudWatch
CI/CD	GitHub Actions, AWS IAM OIDC authentication
Automated testing	JUnit 5, Mockito, Spring MockMvc, Testcontainers (MySQL)
API testing	Postman


Services
Service	Responsibility	Local port
api-gateway	Client entry point, JWT filtering, and request routing	8765
user-service	Registration, login, and user management	8084
order-service	Order creation, retrieval, and status changes	8081
payment-service	Simulated payment processing and payment events	8082
notification-service	Persisted notifications and customer emails	8083
naming-server	Eureka service discovery	8761
spring-cloud-config-server	Centralized configuration	8888


Each business service has its own logical MySQL database. RabbitMQ handles asynchronous communication between services.
Architecture
```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Client[Client / Postman] --> Gateway[Spring Cloud API Gateway]
    Gateway --> Users[User Service]
    Gateway --> Orders[Order Service]
    Gateway --> Payments[Payment Service]
    Gateway --> Notifications[Notification Service]

    Orders -->|OrderCreatedEvent| MQ[(RabbitMQ)]
    MQ -->|OrderCreatedEvent| Payments
    MQ -->|OrderCreatedEvent| Notifications
    Payments -->|PaymentCompletedEvent| MQ
    MQ -->|PaymentCompletedEvent| Orders
    MQ -->|PaymentCompletedEvent| Notifications

    Users --> UDB[(User DB)]
    Orders --> ODB[(Order DB)]
    Payments --> PDB[(Payment DB)]
    Notifications --> NDB[(Notification DB)]
    Notifications --> SES[Amazon SES / SMTP]
    SES --> Inbox[Customer Email Inbox]

    Config[Spring Cloud Config] -.-> Gateway
    Config -.-> Users
    Config -.-> Orders
    Config -.-> Payments
    Config -.-> Notifications
    Eureka[Eureka Discovery] -.-> Gateway
    Eureka -.-> Users
    Eureka -.-> Orders
    Eureka -.-> Payments
    Eureka -.-> Notifications
```
The diagram represents application-level interactions. In AWS, application containers run on ECS Fargate; Docker images are stored in ECR, MySQL is hosted on RDS, and application logs are available in CloudWatch.
Key Features
- User registration and login with JWT-based authentication.
- Central API Gateway routing and protected endpoints.
- Eureka service discovery and centralized configuration.
- Order creation, lookup, and automatic status updates.
- Event-driven simulated payment processing with SUCCESS or FAILED results.
- Persistent order and payment notifications.
- Transactional customer emails for order receipt and payment results.
- Independent databases for business services.
- Local multi-container orchestration with Docker Compose.
- AWS ECS Fargate deployments and automated service-specific GitHub Actions workflows.
- CloudWatch logs for troubleshooting application and email delivery failures.
- 29 automated Order Service tests covering service logic, REST controllers, MySQL repositories, and application context startup.
Authentication Flow
Register user → user-service hashes password and stores user
Login → user-service validates credentials and issues JWT
Authenticated API call → client sends Authorization: Bearer <JWT_TOKEN>
API Gateway validates JWT → routes request to the target service
Event-Driven Order Processing
1. Client creates an order through the API Gateway.
2. order-service stores the order with status PENDING.
3. order-service publishes OrderCreatedEvent to RabbitMQ.
4. payment-service consumes the event and simulates a payment result.
5. notification-service consumes the order event, saves a notification,
   and sends an "Order Received" email through Amazon SES.
6. payment-service publishes PaymentCompletedEvent.
7. order-service consumes the payment event and updates the order:
      SUCCESS → CONFIRMED
      FAILED  → PAYMENT_FAILED
8. notification-service consumes the payment event, saves a notification,
   and sends a payment-result email through Amazon SES.
   Important: Payment outcomes are randomly simulated for demonstration. The application does not integrate with a real payment provider.
AWS Deployment
The application has been deployed in AWS us-east-1 (N. Virginia).
AWS service	Purpose
Amazon ECS Fargate	Runs containerized Spring Boot services without managing EC2 hosts
Amazon ECR	Stores versioned application Docker images
Amazon RDS for MySQL	Hosts relational application data
Amazon SES	Sends transactional emails from the verified collins-dev.com domain
Amazon CloudWatch Logs	Collects service logs and email failure diagnostics
AWS IAM / OIDC	Allows GitHub Actions to authenticate to AWS without long-lived AWS access keys in workflows


Cloud Deployment Workflow
Push changes to main
        ↓
GitHub Actions (service-specific workflow)
        ↓
Run Maven verification and automated tests (Order Service workflow)
        ↓
Build Java application and Docker image
        ↓
Push image to Amazon ECR
        ↓
Update ECS service and start new deployment
        ↓
Wait for ECS service to stabilize
        ↓
Inspect logs and test API endpoints
Five service-specific deployment workflows are configured under .github/workflows/:
- deploy-user-service.yml
- deploy-order-service.yml
- deploy-payment-service.yml
- deploy-notification-service.yml
- deploy-api-gateway.yml
  These workflows support manual dispatch and path-filtered pushes to main, allowing application changes to deploy their corresponding services.
  Deployment note: The gateway has been tested using an ECS task's public IP and port 8765. ECS task public IPs can change after redeployments, so any previously observed IP should not be treated as a permanent application URL. A stable HTTPS endpoint behind a load balancer and custom domain is a future improvement.
Amazon SES Sending Restrictions
The collins-dev.com sender domain is verified in Amazon SES. The AWS SES account was still in sandbox mode during testing, so recipients also needed to be verified in the same AWS region. Production access must be approved by AWS before sending to arbitrary unverified recipients.
Verified End-to-End Tests
The deployed system was exercised through Postman and the API Gateway. The latest verified order-processing run (October 28, 2026) confirmed:
1. API Gateway health endpoint responded successfully on port 8765.
2. GET orders through the gateway returned HTTP 200 OK.
3. An order was created and processed through the asynchronous payment workflow.
4. Payment Service persisted payment #4 for order #4, amount $159.99, with status SUCCESS, then published a payment event.
5. Notification Service persisted notification records and logged successful email sends for the order workflow.
6. Retrieving order #4 through the gateway returned HTTP 200 OK with final status CONFIRMED.
This is a manual Postman end-to-end verification, not an automated end-to-end test suite. The notification logs show two successful email-send events; they do not by themselves establish exactly-once delivery.
Automated Order Service Tests
The Order Service Maven build passed 29 of 29 automated tests locally, and the corresponding GitHub Actions deployment workflow completed successfully.
Test category	Tests	Tooling
